<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naneghat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naneghat is a mountain pass in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junnar">Junnar</a>. It was a major trade route in the Satavahana period, and a cave at the top bears the inscription of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naganika">Naganika</a>, which records the Vedic sacrifices of her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satakarni-i">Satakarni I</a> and is one of the earliest Satavahana records. It includes some of the earliest known Brahmi numerals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naneghat.jpg" alt="Naneghat" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2nd_century_BCE_Nanaghat_Sanskrit_Inscriptions_Maharashtra_India_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
